[38;5;252m[1m## What the C actually does[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc_src/src/main.c[0m uses digraphs and [38;5;10m<iso646.h>[0m spellings; decoded it is [38;5;10mscanf("%d",&x); scanf("%d",&y);[0m then [38;5;10mprintf("%d", x | ~y); puts("")[0m. There are no [38;5;10mif[0ms or early returns, so the entire input-class space comes from scanf outcomes (success / matching failure / EOF), glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m conversion limits, and the fact that all [38;5;10mscanf[0m/[38;5;10mprintf[0m/[38;5;10mputs[0m return values are discarded.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Two real mismatches, both on the write side[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [1mstdout on [38;5;10m/dev/full[0m[22m — C exits 0 with empty stderr (the failed flush at [38;5;10mexit()[0m is unchecked). Rust used [38;5;10mwrite!(...).unwrap()[0m, so it aborted with status 134 and a panic message on stderr. Fixed by discarding write/flush results.[0m[0m
2. [1mstdout is a pipe with no reader[22m — C is killed by [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m (status 141). The Rust runtime sets [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m to [38;5;10mSIG_IGN[0m before [38;5;10mmain[0m, so the write returned [38;5;10mEPIPE[0m and the [38;5;10munwrap[0m aborted (134). Fixed by restoring [38;5;10mSIG_DFL[0m for [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m via a bare [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m declaration — no new dependency.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Neither is reachable through stdin bytes alone, which is why running the binary the way a shell does mattered.[0m[0m
